$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db list
yubikey-db.sh: error: cannot use PostgreSQL database <T>_db: connection to server <SERVER> failed: FATAL:  database "<T>_db" does not exist
[exit 1]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init-pg --admin <T>_op
Roles ready: <T>_admin (owner) and <T>_radiator (Radiator login); members of <T>_admin: <T>_op
Database ready: PostgreSQL database <T>_db (owner <T>_admin, Radiator role <T>_radiator)
Next steps:
  - Radiator on this host: with Ubuntu's default peer authentication on the
    local socket, Radiator must run as the OS user "<T>_radiator" and needs no password.
  - Radiator on another host: add a line like this to pg_hba.conf, above the
    generic "host all all" lines, reload PostgreSQL, and give <T>_radiator a password
    with: yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init-pg --set-password
        hostssl  <T>_db  <T>_radiator  <radiator-ip>/32  scram-sha-256
  - Print the Radiator configuration: yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db config
[exit 0]

# columns
column_name | data_type | max_length | is_nullable | column_default
username | character varying | 255 | NO | 
password | text |  | NO | ''::text
active | smallint |  | NO | 1
created | timestamp with time zone |  | NO | now()
accessed | timestamp with time zone |  | YES | 
tokenid | character varying | 60 | NO | 
userid | character varying | 60 | NO | 
secret | character varying | 60 | NO | 
counter | integer |  | NO | 0
low | integer |  | NO | 0
high | integer |  | NO | 0
# constraints (NOT NULL is shown in the columns above)
conname | definition
yubikeys_active_check | CHECK ((active = ANY (ARRAY[0, 1])))
yubikeys_pkey | PRIMARY KEY (userid)
yubikeys_tokenid_key | UNIQUE (tokenid)
# indexes
indexname
yubikeys_pkey
yubikeys_tokenid_key
yubikeys_username
# owners
database_owner | table_owner
<T>_admin | <T>_admin
# privileges for roles other than the owner
grantee | privilege_type | columns
<T>_radiator | SELECT | active, counter, high, low, password, secret, tokenid, userid, username
<T>_radiator | UPDATE | accessed, counter, high, low
grantee | table_level_privilege
database_acl
{<T>_admin=CTc/<T>_admin,<T>_radiator=c/<T>_admin}
# members of the admin role (test roles only)
member
<T>_op

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init-pg
Roles ready: <T>_admin (owner) and <T>_radiator (Radiator login)
Database ready: PostgreSQL database <T>_db (owner <T>_admin, Radiator role <T>_radiator)
Next steps:
  - Radiator on this host: with Ubuntu's default peer authentication on the
    local socket, Radiator must run as the OS user "<T>_radiator" and needs no password.
  - Radiator on another host: add a line like this to pg_hba.conf, above the
    generic "host all all" lines, reload PostgreSQL, and give <T>_radiator a password
    with: yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init-pg --set-password
        hostssl  <T>_db  <T>_radiator  <radiator-ip>/32  scram-sha-256
  - Print the Radiator configuration: yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db config
[exit 0]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init
Database ready: PostgreSQL database <T>_db (owner <T>_admin, Radiator role <T>_radiator)
[exit 0]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db list
 username | public_id | active | created | accessed | counter 
----------+-----------+--------+---------+----------+---------
[exit 0]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init --bogus
yubikey-db.sh: error: init: unknown option '--bogus'
[exit 1]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init-pg --bogus
yubikey-db.sh: error: init-pg: unknown option '--bogus'
[exit 1]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init-pg --admin Bad-Login
yubikey-db.sh: error: invalid login role 'Bad-Login' (use lowercase letters, digits and _, starting with a letter or _)
[exit 1]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d bad-name init-pg
yubikey-db.sh: error: invalid database name 'bad-name' (use lowercase letters, digits and _, starting with a letter or _)
[exit 1]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_db init-pg --set-password
yubikey-db.sh: error: init-pg: --set-password needs a terminal to prompt on
[exit 1]

$ yubikey-db.sh -b pg -d <T>_empty list
yubikey-db.sh: error: PostgreSQL database <T>_empty has no yubikeys table; run 'yubikey-db.sh -b pg init-pg' first
[exit 1]

